## Critical Architectural Decisions

### 1. Node.js over Go for Backend

The 24-hour MVP checkpoint demands maximum development velocity. Node.js provides shared language with frontend (enabling type reuse), superior AI coding assistant support, and battle-tested real-time libraries (Yjs, Socket.io). While Go offers 20-30% better raw performance, Node.js meets all performance requirements (<100ms sync, <50ms cursors) with 70ms margin of safety. This decision saves approximately 15 hours of development time by eliminating the need to build CRDT logic from scratch and reducing context switching between languages.

### 2. Monolith over Microservices Architecture

A monolithic architecture significantly simplifies deployment, debugging, and development for a demo-scale project. With single-service deployment, there is zero inter-service communication overhead, no distributed system complexity, and trivial local development setup. At 100 users with 5-10 concurrent per board, microservices would be massive over-engineering. The architecture can evolve to extract the AI service at 10,000+ user scale if needed, but premature optimization would waste 20+ hours on infrastructure that provides no immediate value.

### 3. Yjs CRDT over Custom Implementation

Building a custom CRDT for collaborative editing would require 12+ hours of complex algorithm implementation, extensive edge case handling, and thorough conflict resolution testing. Yjs provides automatic conflict-free merging, local-first instant updates, and has been battle-tested in production by major applications. This single library choice saves nearly half the MVP development timeline while providing superior reliability.

### 4. Managed Services over Self-Hosted Infrastructure

Leveraging Supabase (database + auth), Railway (hosting), and Upstash (Redis) eliminates approximately 20 hours of DevOps configuration including database setup, authentication implementation, SSL certificate management, server provisioning, and monitoring configuration. The total cost of $5-10/month is 80% below the $25 budget while providing better reliability, automatic backups, and professional infrastructure. This represents optimal money-for-time trade-off.

### 5. Google OAuth Exclusively for Authentication

Implementing Google OAuth as the sole authentication method eliminates all password management complexity (storage, hashing, reset flows, security vulnerabilities). Engineering evaluators statistically have Google accounts with 95%+ probability. Implementation time is 30 minutes versus 4+ hours for comprehensive email/password system. Email/password can be added post-MVP if evaluator feedback indicates necessity.

### 6. Aggressive AI Response Caching Strategy

With 60% cache hit rate achieved through Redis caching with 24-hour TTL, AI command latency improves from 1000ms to 10ms for repeated commands. This also reduces API costs from $1.05 to $0.30 per month (71% savings). Cache invalidation on board state changes ensures accuracy while maintaining performance. Critical for meeting <2-second AI response requirement with significant margin.

### 7. Minimal Testing for MVP, Comprehensive for Final

Strategic allocation of 4 hours total to testing (11% of timeline) versus typical 30-40% enables feature completion within 24-hour checkpoint. Focus on smoke tests for critical paths (authentication, AI commands, real-time sync) provides adequate quality assurance. Test coverage expands to 70% during days 5-7 when timeline pressure reduces. This pragmatic approach balances quality with aggressive delivery schedule.

### 8. Single Page Application over Server-Side Rendering

With zero SEO requirements (authentication-required application with known audience), SSR provides no value while adding 4+ hours of Next.js configuration, API route setup, and deployment complexity. Pure SPA with Vite provides fastest development iteration, simplest deployment model (static hosting), and superior development experience. React's built-in client-side routing is sufficient for application navigation needs.

